NAME
       expr - evaluate a simple expression

SYNOPSIS
       expr ARGUMENT OPERATOR ARGUMENT

DESCRIPTION
       expr works out an expression and prints the result. Each number and
       operator must be a separate argument (with spaces in between), and
       characters the shell treats specially, such as * < >, must be quoted
       or escaped.

       Operators: + - \* / % for whole-number arithmetic; = != < <= > >= for
       comparisons, which print 1 (true) or 0 (false).

       It comes from older shells; in bash, $(( )) and [[ ]] are simpler.

EXAMPLES
           expr 7 + 5             prints 12
           expr 7 \* 5            prints 35
           count=$(expr $count + 1)

SEE ALSO
       bash(1), bc(1), test(1)
